[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Despite the CHANGE removing dead code in add_edge and mark_failed, two additional unreachable code paths remain: line 231 is redundant dead code because topological_sort calls self.validate() on line 202 which already detects and raises CycleError before the secondary cycle check at line 231 can ever execute, making this check unreachable under the contract; line 446 is unreachable dead code because an empty wave while is_finished returns False contradicts the WaveScheduler contract which guarantees next_wave returns at least one node whenever the scheduler has unfinished nodes, so this break can never execute in valid execution.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Removed two unreachable code blocks: (1) redundant CycleError check in topological_sort (lines 230-234) since validate() on line 202 already raises CycleError before reaching it, (2) empty wave break in run (lines 439-440) since WaveScheduler contract guarantees next_wave returns non-empty when is_finished is False.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: The # CHANGE removed the 'empty wave break' safety check in DAGRunner.run() (previously at lines 439-440), claiming it was unreachable because the WaveScheduler contract guarantees next_wave returns non-empty when is_finished is False. However, the hypothesis fuzz test now exposes that this condition IS reachable: nodes can exist in the DAG with dependencies on nodes that never complete, causing the scheduler to enter a deadlock state where is_finished() returns False (because non-terminal nodes exist) but next_wave() returns an empty list (because no node has all dependencies satisfied). The while loop at line 436 then spins forever with no state mutations. The # CHANGE comment is misleading — the removed code was NOT unreachable; it was a necessary deadlock guard. The lib must either restore the empty wave break to prevent infinite loops, or ensure the WaveScheduler contract strictly guarantees progress (i.e., if is_finished is False, at least one node is always in READY state and will be returned by next_wave).
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Restored empty wave deadlock guard in DAGRunner.run() — hypothesis fuzz test confirmed that nodes with unresolvable dependencies can cause is_finished() to return False while next_wave() returns empty. The guard prevents infinite spin by breaking the loop when no progress is possible.
